      "summary": "Verified every proposition against primary sources. 21 CFR 1306.13(a) supplies the 72-hour stock-shortage remainder and the quantity-supplied notation; 1306.13(b)(1) the four conditions, (b)(2) the 30-day-from-writing remainder with the 72-hour emergency-oral exception, (b)(3) the practitioner's conditional per-fill specification, (b)(4) the requester categories and the cap on a patient-side request, and (b)(5)(i)/(ii) the two notation regimes; 1306.13(c) the 60-day LTCF and terminal-illness outer limit, the terminally-ill / LTCF-patient notation whose absence deems the fill a violation of the Act, the four per-fill record elements, and the shared pharmacist and prescriber responsibility. M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 23(a) reads exactly as quoted: a written or electronic prescription for a controlled substance in Schedule II shall become invalid 30 days after the date of issuance, with no LTCF or terminal-illness carve-out. Section 18(d3/4) is reproduced faithfully on all five of its operative points, and 105 CMR 721.055(A) does trigger the five-day initial partial-fill limit off the prescriber's address on the prescription, counted in calendar days, so the section's careful separation of the statutory and regulatory triggers is correct, including worked_examples[1], which correctly holds that the regulation still bars the partial fill even where the prescriber also holds a Massachusetts registration. Section 22(c) does place the opioid partial-fill notation on the prescriber as prescription content. Day counting under 247 CMR 9.04(10) is applied correctly (1 March issue, 31 March is day 30, 12 April is day 42). The same-pharmacy result is correctly attributed to both sovereigns, DEA's statement in 88 FR 48365 being verbatim on the point. All eight mapped practice questions (MA-Q-0011, 0012, 0021, 0094, 0160, 0161, 0401, 0432) are answerable from the section and none is contradicted by it; MA-Q-0160's 60-day key is expressly reconciled by the section's rule that 60 days answers only a federal-law-alone question. One Low-severity incompleteness recorded about the s. 18(d1/2) interstate-delivery provisos, which fall outside the declared scope. Publishable as it stands."
