      "summary": "The four conditions of 21 CFR 1306.13(b)(1) are reproduced exactly, including the section's central point that (b)(1)(i) asks only for an absence of State prohibition rather than affirmative State permission. The reading that (b)(2) times only the remaining portions and not the first dispensing is correct on the regulation's face, and the Massachusetts limits the section puts in that gap are correct: s. 23(a)'s 30-day invalidity, 247 CMR 9.04(10)'s counting rule, s. 18(d3/4)'s five-day limit on the initial partial dispensing of a prescription filled under s. 18(d) or s. 18(d1/2), and 105 CMR 721.055(A)'s address-based trigger. The refill comparison is exact: 21 CFR 1306.22(a) states six months and five refills for Schedules III and IV only, 247 CMR 9.04(13) states the identical pair for Schedule V only, and 247 CMR 9.04(15) removes 9.04(13), along with 9.04(4), (5), (6), (9), (10), (12) and (14), from institutional sterile compounding pharmacies. Attribution to sovereigns is right throughout, including the same-pharmacy remainder result, which both sovereigns reach: DEA stated in 88 FR 48365 that no DEA regulation permits a partially-filled controlled substance prescription to be transferred from one DEA-registered pharmacy to another for dispensing of the remaining portion. Both mapped practice questions (MA-Q-0021, MA-Q-0401) are answerable from the section and neither is contradicted; MA-Q-0401's key, that the five-day limit reaches only the out-of-state pathways, is directly supported on its Massachusetts-prescriber facts. One Low-severity finding records the section's own interpretive extension of 1306.13(b)(1)(ii) to a missing s. 22(c) notation, which is textually supported and operationally safe. Publishable as it stands."
