      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Every figure, actor and record requirement checks out against the text. 21 CFR 1306.22(a) bars filling as well as refilling more than six months after issue for Schedules III and IV, so the section's insistence that the six-month bar is not a refill-only limit is correct; 247 CMR 9.04(13) supplies the identical pair for Schedule V, which the federal provision does not reach, and 247 CMR 9.04(15) removes 9.04(10), (12) and (13) from institutional sterile compounding pharmacies. The two federal transfer pathways are distinguished correctly: 1306.25(a) is one-time only for refill dispensing of Schedule III-V prescription information with the shared real-time online database alternative, (b)(1) requires direct pharmacist-to-pharmacist communication, (b)(2)-(b)(5) match the VOID, transfer and history entries the section lists, (c) sets two years from the date of last refill, (d) holds shared-record pharmacies to the manual information requirements, and (e) conditions the whole procedure on State law; 1306.08(e) reaches Schedules II-V for initial dispensing between retail pharmacies at the patient's request on a one-time basis with authorized refills travelling along, (f)(1)-(f)(6) require the electronic, unaltered, pharmacist-to-pharmacist form and the transfer entries, (g) supplies the State-law condition and (h) two years from the date of transfer at both pharmacies. The claim that a never-dispensed paper prescription has no federal transfer pathway matches DEA's own account in 88 FR 48365. The Schedule VI analysis is sound: M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 2(a) and 105 CMR 700.002(A)-(F) put Massachusetts Schedules I-V on the federal lists and Schedule VI on all other prescription drugs, so 1306.25 cannot reach a Schedule VI drug; 247 CMR 9.14(4)(a)-(c) and 9.04(12) say exactly what the section says, including the 14-day or smallest-unit-of-use professional-judgment supply; s. 23(f) reaches Schedule VI through the s. 1 definition of controlled substance; and 247 CMR 8.04(4)(d) permits a certified pharmacy technician to perform prescription transfers between pharmacies for prescriptions issued for controlled substances in Schedule VI only, with the approval of the pharmacist on duty. The four worked examples reach the right result on their own arithmetic. Both mapped practice questions (MA-Q-0120, MA-Q-0397) are answerable from the section and neither is contradicted. Nothing wrong found; publishable as it stands."
